<h2>The problem Section 29A created, and solves</h2>
<p>The time-limit regime for domestic arbitral awards is deliberately strict: twelve months from completion of pleadings, a consensual six-month stretch, and thereafter extension only by the court, which may impose terms and even substitute arbitrators while doing so. The strictness is the point — it is what distinguishes modern Indian arbitration from the leisurely proceedings the amendment era set out to end. But strict timelines create a strategic temptation for a party that expects to lose: disengage, decline consent to the six-month extension, ignore the extension petition, and hope the clock does the work no defence could. Orders like the present one close that route.</p>

<h2>What the Court did</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Service verified.</strong> The respondent was served with the extension petition; the record showed no participation and no response.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Ex parte consideration.</strong> Rather than adjourn into the very delay the statute abhors, the Court took up the petition ex parte.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Stage assessed.</strong> The arbitration stood at the evidence stage — substantial progress, substantial work remaining.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Mandate extended.</strong> Twelve months from the order, for completing proceedings and rendering the award.</div>
</div>

<h2>How courts weigh extension petitions</h2>
<p>The jurisprudence under Section 29A has settled into a pragmatic shape. Extension is the norm where the proceedings are genuinely advancing and the delay is explicable — voluminous evidence, interlocutory skirmishes, scheduling realities of busy tribunals. Courts look at the stage reached, the conduct of the parties (a petitioner responsible for obstruction gets a cooler reception), and the time realistically needed to finish. The power to refuse exists for arbitrations that have died in all but name, not as a reward for respondents who starve a living arbitration of consent and attendance. Where one party is absent despite service, the analysis simplifies further: the only interest weighing against extension belongs to someone who declined to assert it.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Bearing on extension</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Stage of the arbitration</td><td>Advanced stages (evidence, arguments) strongly favour extension — sunk adjudicative cost</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cause of delay</td><td>Explicable procedural causes favour extension; obstruction by the applicant invites terms or costs</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conduct of the respondent</td><td>Non-participation despite service removes practical opposition and justifies ex parte disposal</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Time sought</td><td>Calibrated to remaining work; twelve months is common for arbitrations still in evidence</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>File before expiry.</strong> An extension petition filed while the mandate subsists keeps the tribunal functioning; the proceedings can continue during the petition&rsquo;s pendency, and the award&rsquo;s validity is not left to retrospective repair.</p>
  <p><strong>Prove service scrupulously.</strong> Ex parte extension rests entirely on the service record. Dispatch proof, tracking and affidavits of service should accompany the petition.</p>
  <p><strong>Ask for a realistic period.</strong> Courts respond better to a reasoned estimate — witnesses remaining, hearings scheduled — than to a bare request for the maximum.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
